1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,520
In 1854, cholera was killing people in one

2
00:00:03,600 --> 00:00:04,840
London neighbourhood.

3
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:07,200
John Snow marked the deaths on a street

4
00:00:07,280 --> 00:00:10,000
map, and the deaths clustered around one water

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,280
pump on Broad Street.

6
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,640
He didn't know which microbe caused cholera, but

7
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:17,360
the map showed how it travelled: in drinking

8
00:00:17,440 --> 00:00:18,280
water.

9
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,560
So how does a pathogen get from one

10
00:00:20,640 --> 00:00:23,040
host into the next, and how does knowing

11
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:25,120
its route tell us where to stop it?

12
00:00:27,134 --> 00:00:29,800
To spread, a pathogen has to leave one

13
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:32,733
host, travel, and get into a new host.

14
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:35,133
The way it travels is called its mode

15
00:00:35,214 --> 00:00:38,973
of transmission, and there are three: direct contact,

16
00:00:39,094 --> 00:00:41,933
indirect contact, and vector transmission.

17
00:00:42,254 --> 00:00:44,493
Each step is a link in a chain,

18
00:00:44,614 --> 00:00:46,413
and every link is a place where the

19
00:00:46,494 --> 00:00:47,613
chain can break.

20
00:00:49,634 --> 00:00:52,673
In direct contact, the pathogen passes straight from

21
00:00:52,754 --> 00:00:55,473
one host to another, with nothing in between.

22
00:00:55,954 --> 00:00:58,353
Tinea spreads by skin touching skin.

23
00:00:58,834 --> 00:01:02,153
HIV passes in blood and other body fluids.

24
00:01:02,354 --> 00:01:04,673
And large droplets from a cough or sneeze

25
00:01:04,754 --> 00:01:07,393
fall within a metre or two, so close-range

26
00:01:07,474 --> 00:01:10,353
droplet spread, a main route for influenza, is

27
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:12,513
usually grouped with direct contact.

28
00:01:14,534 --> 00:01:18,453
In indirect contact, something non-living carries the pathogen

29
00:01:18,534 --> 00:01:19,733
between hosts.

30
00:01:19,974 --> 00:01:22,453
It might be a contaminated object, called a

31
00:01:22,534 --> 00:01:25,933
fomite, like a door handle carrying norovirus.

32
00:01:26,214 --> 00:01:28,213
It might be water, like the Broad Street

33
00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:31,973
pump carrying cholera, or food, like undercooked chicken

34
00:01:32,054 --> 00:01:33,253
carrying Salmonella.

35
00:01:33,894 --> 00:01:36,933
Or it might be the air: tuberculosis and

36
00:01:37,014 --> 00:01:40,053
measles travel in tiny particles that hang in

37
00:01:40,094 --> 00:01:42,613
a room after an infected person leaves.

38
00:01:44,634 --> 00:01:47,993
In vector transmission, a living organism, usually an

39
00:01:48,154 --> 00:01:50,873
insect or a tick, carries the pathogen from

40
00:01:50,954 --> 00:01:52,073
host to host.

41
00:01:52,714 --> 00:01:56,793
Anopheles mosquitoes carry Plasmodium, the protozoan that causes

42
00:01:56,834 --> 00:01:57,513
malaria.

43
00:01:58,154 --> 00:02:01,833
Plasmodium develops inside the mosquito, so the mosquito

44
00:02:01,914 --> 00:02:03,673
is part of its life cycle.

45
00:02:03,994 --> 00:02:07,033
Careful, though: a door handle isn't a vector.

46
00:02:07,434 --> 00:02:09,033
A vector is alive.

47
00:02:09,434 --> 00:02:12,073
A contaminated object is a fomite.

48
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:16,900
Now, how are pathogens built for this?

49
00:02:17,220 --> 00:02:18,820
Start with getting in.

50
00:02:19,140 --> 00:02:23,060
Many bacteria have fimbriae, short hair-like threads that

51
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:26,100
grip molecules on host cells, so the bacteria

52
00:02:26,180 --> 00:02:28,780
aren't washed away by mucus or urine.

53
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:32,580
Viruses have surface proteins that fit receptor molecules

54
00:02:32,660 --> 00:02:34,180
on particular host cells.

55
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:38,420
The COVID-19 virus's spike protein binds a receptor

56
00:02:38,500 --> 00:02:41,140
on cells lining the airways, and that lets

57
00:02:41,180 --> 00:02:42,580
the virus get inside.

58
00:02:44,600 --> 00:02:47,080
So both grip molecules on the host cell's

59
00:02:47,160 --> 00:02:48,000
surface.

60
00:02:48,280 --> 00:02:51,480
The difference is what comes next: the bacterium

61
00:02:51,560 --> 00:02:54,360
can multiply on that surface, while the virus

62
00:02:54,520 --> 00:02:56,560
has to get inside the cell.

63
00:02:57,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Bigger pathogens hold on differently.

64
00:02:59,480 --> 00:03:01,560
The pork tapeworm's head has a ring of

65
00:03:01,640 --> 00:03:04,200
hooks and four suckers, which anchor it to

66
00:03:04,280 --> 00:03:06,520
the gut wall, so it isn't swept out

67
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:07,880
with digested food.

68
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:11,580
Now, getting out.

69
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:15,100
Influenza multiplies in the airway lining and triggers

70
00:03:15,180 --> 00:03:18,380
coughing and sneezing, which throw out droplets full

71
00:03:18,460 --> 00:03:19,420
of virus.

72
00:03:19,740 --> 00:03:22,860
Plasmodium ends up in the mosquito's salivary glands,

73
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:25,420
so it's injected with saliva into the skin

74
00:03:25,500 --> 00:03:26,780
when the mosquito bites.

75
00:03:27,500 --> 00:03:29,980
The tapeworm's eggs leave in faeces and become

76
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:33,580
cysts in pig muscle, so undercooked pork carries

77
00:03:33,620 --> 00:03:34,060
it on.

78
00:03:34,620 --> 00:03:37,100
And tobacco mosaic virus has a coat so

79
00:03:37,180 --> 00:03:40,060
tough that it survives on pruning tools, which

80
00:03:40,140 --> 00:03:41,820
carry it from plant to plant.

81
00:03:43,834 --> 00:03:46,473
Exams ask you to compare these, so keep

82
00:03:46,634 --> 00:03:47,953
two jobs apart.

83
00:03:48,234 --> 00:03:51,033
An entry adaptation gets a pathogen in and

84
00:03:51,114 --> 00:03:53,753
holds it where it enters, like fimbriae or

85
00:03:53,834 --> 00:03:54,313
hooks.

86
00:03:54,874 --> 00:03:57,833
A transmission adaptation gets it out and across

87
00:03:57,914 --> 00:04:00,473
to the next host, like triggering a cough

88
00:04:00,554 --> 00:04:02,473
or riding in mosquito saliva.

89
00:04:03,114 --> 00:04:05,593
Name the feature, say what it does, then

90
00:04:05,674 --> 00:04:06,873
link it to its job.

91
00:04:08,900 --> 00:04:10,980
Knowing the route tells you where to break

92
00:04:11,060 --> 00:04:11,940
the chain.

93
00:04:12,260 --> 00:04:16,660
Direct contact: barriers and distance, like gloves, condoms

94
00:04:16,820 --> 00:04:18,540
and staying home when sick.

95
00:04:18,820 --> 00:04:23,060
Indirect contact: treat the carrier, by chlorinating water,

96
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,980
cooking food properly, cleaning surfaces and ventilating rooms.

97
00:04:27,540 --> 00:04:31,540
Vectors: bed nets, repellent, and draining standing water

98
00:04:31,620 --> 00:04:33,100
where mosquitoes breed.

99
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:35,700
Snow had the pump's handle taken off, which

100
00:04:35,780 --> 00:04:36,820
broke the water link.

101
00:04:37,300 --> 00:04:39,860
Get the route wrong, and scrubbing surfaces does

102
00:04:39,940 --> 00:04:42,180
little against tuberculosis in the air.

103
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:46,600
So name the route first, and the control

104
00:04:46,760 --> 00:04:47,600
follows.

105
00:04:47,880 --> 00:04:49,880
Every control measure breaks a link.

106
00:04:50,280 --> 00:04:53,400
A mask breaks droplets, clean water breaks the

107
00:04:53,480 --> 00:04:55,640
water route, and a bed net breaks the

108
00:04:55,760 --> 00:04:56,680
mosquito's.

109
00:04:58,700 --> 00:05:00,460
Two exam questions.

110
00:05:00,780 --> 00:05:01,260
First.

111
00:05:01,900 --> 00:05:05,340
After heavy rain, Ross River virus cases rose

112
00:05:05,380 --> 00:05:06,620
in a Queensland town.

113
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,660
The virus is carried by mosquitoes that breed

114
00:05:09,740 --> 00:05:12,620
in standing water, and it isn't passed directly

115
00:05:12,700 --> 00:05:13,580
between people.

116
00:05:14,380 --> 00:05:17,500
Measure A isolates infected people in hospital.

117
00:05:18,060 --> 00:05:21,100
Measure B drains standing water and treats it

118
00:05:21,180 --> 00:05:22,060
with larvicide.

119
00:05:22,780 --> 00:05:26,060
Identify the mode of transmission, and justify which

120
00:05:26,140 --> 00:05:28,460
measure would better reduce new cases.

121
00:05:29,100 --> 00:05:30,380
Pause here to answer.

122
00:05:41,600 --> 00:05:45,200
It's vector transmission, because mosquitoes carry the virus

123
00:05:45,280 --> 00:05:46,320
between hosts.

124
00:05:46,880 --> 00:05:50,240
Measure B is better: draining and treating standing

125
00:05:50,320 --> 00:05:54,160
water removes where mosquitoes breed, so fewer mosquitoes

126
00:05:54,240 --> 00:05:55,840
carry the virus to new people.

127
00:05:56,400 --> 00:05:59,120
Choosing isolation is the classic mistake.

128
00:05:59,600 --> 00:06:02,640
The virus isn't passed directly between people, so

129
00:06:02,720 --> 00:06:05,600
isolation targets a link it doesn't use.

130
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:08,560
Second.

131
00:06:08,640 --> 00:06:12,320
The cholera bacterium's pili, hair-like threads, help it

132
00:06:12,440 --> 00:06:15,360
cling in clumps to the small intestine's lining,

133
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:18,400
and it causes watery diarrhoea that releases huge

134
00:06:18,480 --> 00:06:20,400
numbers of bacteria.

135
00:06:20,480 --> 00:06:23,360
Influenza virus has a surface protein that binds

136
00:06:23,480 --> 00:06:27,840
receptors on airway cells, and infection triggers coughing.

137
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:30,480
Compare how these two pathogens are adapted for

138
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:34,240
entry into, and transmission between, hosts.

139
00:06:34,400 --> 00:06:35,520
Pause here to answer.

140
00:06:46,734 --> 00:06:50,413
Similar: both hold on where they enter, pili

141
00:06:50,454 --> 00:06:52,413
on the gut lining and the protein on

142
00:06:52,574 --> 00:06:55,373
airway cells, and both cause a symptom that

143
00:06:55,454 --> 00:06:56,253
releases them.

144
00:06:56,894 --> 00:07:00,333
Different: cholera leaves in diarrhoea and is swallowed

145
00:07:00,414 --> 00:07:04,093
in water, whereas influenza leaves in coughed droplets

146
00:07:04,174 --> 00:07:05,773
and is breathed in.

147
00:07:06,334 --> 00:07:09,373
Writing two separate descriptions, with no similarity or

148
00:07:09,854 --> 00:07:11,613
difference, is the classic mistake.

149
00:07:13,634 --> 00:07:16,433
So for any disease, trace how the pathogen

150
00:07:16,594 --> 00:07:20,593
leaves, travels and enters, sort each adaptation by

151
00:07:20,674 --> 00:07:23,073
its job, and break the link its route

152
00:07:23,154 --> 00:07:23,873
depends on.

153
00:07:24,914 --> 00:07:27,313
Next, a classroom practical that models how an

154
00:07:27,474 --> 00:07:29,233
infection spreads through a group.

155
00:07:31,234 --> 00:07:32,673
Here are the key notes.

156
00:07:32,914 --> 00:07:34,833
Pause the video if you need more time

157
00:07:34,914 --> 00:07:35,713
to copy them.
